# How this deck was made (W3C PROV-O): researched card by card from the sources below,
# each card checked against at least two of them, then in the review rounds below.
<#compilation>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T12:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:used <https://www.wikidata.org/> ,
              <https://www.unicode.org/Public/18.0.0/ucd/UnicodeData.txt> ,
              <https://www.iso.org/standard/64973.html> ,
              <https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Glossary_of_mathematical_symbols&oldid=1377851125> ,
              <https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Natural_number&oldid=1375965996> ,
              <https://sv.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Lista_%C3%B6ver_matematiska_symboler&oldid=57825007> ,
              <https://sv.wikipedia.org/> ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith <#anton-wiklund> ;
    prov:wasInformedBy <#review-0> ,
                       <#review-1> ,
                       <#review-2> ,
                       <#review-3> ,
                       <#review-4> ,
                       <#review-5> ;
    rdfs:comment "Compiled by Anton Wiklund with the help of AI."@en ,
                 "Sammanställd av Anton Wiklund med hjälp av AI."@sv ,
                 "About this record: the comments on this compilation, its review rounds and its sources are condensed from the deck's dossier, the working record kept while the deck was compiled, from which a provenance report was generated. The dossier's per-card evidence and Wikidata checks, its numbered queries and scripts (its Queries section) and its logs of discrepancies are not part of this release; where the text refers to them, to the dossier or the report, or to findings listed below, it means that record. The builder is the script that built the deck from the dossier and ran its Wikidata checks."@en ,
                 "The cards were checked by machine (the Unicode Character Database, live Wikidata and the app's SHACL and DCAT-AP validators)."@en ,
                 "Candidate symbols: the library editor's brief named about 45 symbols in five groups (logic, sets, relations, operators, constants). For each, Wikidata was searched for the items that carry the symbol as Unicode character (P487) (query 1) and the statements of those items (query 2), which link character items to concepts through notation (P913), depicts (P180), has use (P366) or symbolizes (P4878)."@en ,
                 "ISO 80000-2 as recorded in Wikidata: query 3 listed every Wikidata item described by ISO 80000-2:2019 (Q109490582) with the item number (P958 section, e.g. \"2-6.8\") and the standard's verbal equivalent (P1810 subject named as, e.g. \"B is properly included in A\"). Every card's concept was matched to one of these records where one exists (all but ∝, and ∫ via the indefinite integral item); this is what the evidence calls the ISO item number. The standard's own tables could not be read: the ISO Online Browsing Platform answered 403 (https://www.iso.org/obp/ui/en/#iso:std:iso:80000:-2:ed-2:v1:en), and the publisher's free preview (query 10) ends after clause 4. From the preview only clause 4's sentence on mathematical constants (e, π, i) is used."@en ,
                 "Concepts and labels: query 4 fetched, for the chosen concept items, the English and Swedish labels, Swedish aliases, P487, the notation items with their characters and the ISO records; query 5 the Swedish Wikipedia article title and the character items linked to each concept; query 6 the P487, P913 and P1343 statements that the build checks. While doing so the Query Service was found to return \"ℜ\" (U+211C) as P487 of the set of real numbers (Q1174982) where the Wikidata API returns \"ℝ\" (U+211D) (queries 7 and 8); see round 0."@en ,
                 "Characters: UnicodeData.txt of the Unicode Character Database 18.0.0 was downloaded (query 11) and the script ucd.py (query 12) printed the line of every character used on a front, which fixed the exact code point (for example U+2223 DIVIDES rather than U+007C VERTICAL LINE for \"a ∣ b\", U+2216 SET MINUS rather than the backslash for \"A ∖ B\") and gave the official character name as a check of the meaning."@en ,
                 "Independent check in English: the wikitext of the English Wikipedia \"Glossary of mathematical symbols\" (query 11) was searched with grep_en.py (query 13) and grep (query 14) for the entry of each symbol; each card's meaning agrees with it (∠, π, e and i are not in the glossary). The English Wikipedia article \"Natural number\" (query 15) was read for the ISO convention on 0."@en ,
                 "Swedish: the Swedish Wikipedia \"Lista över matematiska symboler\" (query 11, wikitext extracted by query 20) and Swedish Wikipedia articles on the concepts (svwiki.py, query 16: 46 titles tried, 41 found, two of them redirects to the same article; plain-text extracts with revision ids; grep_sv.py, query 17; in round 3 also \"Skilt från-tecknet\", query 24) were read to confirm the Swedish terms. The Swedish backs are standard Swedish terminology chosen by the compiler: single words or set phrases, not protected expression. Where Wikidata has the term as an sv label or alias, the back follows it (konjunktion, disjunktion, negation, implikation, delmängd, union, snitt, tomma mängden, mindre/större än eller lika med, summa, produkt, kvadratrot, absolutbelopp, golvfunktion, takfunktion, fakultet, sammansatt funktion, nablaoperatorn, imaginära enheten, oändlighet, vinkel, Eulers tal, \"inte lika med-tecknet\" for ≠); the other readings (för alla, det finns, implicerar, om och endast om, är ett element i, äkta delmängd, mängddifferens, kongruent modulo n, delar, proportionell mot, ungefär lika med, plus eller minus) are the usual ones, which the Swedish list and articles only confirmed; no sentences, notes or selection were taken from them. Swedish lower-case style is used throughout (Wikidata's \"Partiell derivata\" is written \"partiell derivata\"), named sets and constants with an adjective take the Swedish double definite form (\"de naturliga talen\", \"den tomma mängden\", \"den imaginära enheten\"), and Swedish decimal commas are used in the Swedish notes."@en ,
                 "Writing the cards: the backs are the compiler's own short wording of the meaning, in the form in which the symbol is read (\"is an element of\", \"för alla\"), with the concept name in brackets where the reading alone is a bare word (\"and (conjunction)\"). Fronts are text in no language (zxx). A symbol is shown in a short expression where it has another common meaning or could be mistaken for another character: \"A ∖ B\", \"a ≡ b (mod n)\", \"a ∣ b\", \"|x|\", \"⌊x⌋\", \"⌈x⌉\", \"n!\", \"g ∘ f\". The constants π, e and i have the front note \"a mathematical constant\" so that e and i are not read as variables (or e as the elementary charge). Notes give a formula, an example or the convention used, in the compiler's own words and with examples of the compiler's own choosing; examples are introduced with \"for example\" / \"till exempel\", and notes that are sentences end with a full stop. Expression fronts get expression readings (\"absolute value of x\", \"floor of x\", \"g composed with f\")."@en ,
                 "Checks: every card has Wikidata checks run by the builder against live Wikidata: the symbol (P487 on the concept or on its character item), the link between character and concept (P913, P180 or P366), a stable label in English or Swedish, and the concept's ISO 80000-2 record (P1343 Q109490582). Checks were left out where Wikidata disagrees with the card or the Query Service returns a different character (⊂ on subset, ℝ, e); see round 0."@en ,
                 "Left out: ⊥ (U+22A5 UP TACK is also the logical constant false, and Unicode has a separate U+27C2 PERPENDICULAR, so the front would be ambiguous between characters and meanings); ≪ and ≫ (no Swedish source for the reading was found beyond the plain translation; the Swedish Wikipedia search for \"mycket mindre än\", query 22, gave 186 hits, the first ten all outside mathematics); ∄, ∃!, ∴, ∵ and ∮ (not in the brief and outside ISO 80000-2's tables as recorded in Wikidata); ≡ alone (identity, congruence or logical equivalence) and the bare vertical bar (absolute value, divides, such that), which appear only in expressions that fix one meaning."@en ,
                 "Selection: 45 symbols in five groups, chosen from the library editor's brief and grouped broadly as ISO 80000-2:2019's clauses (as recorded in Wikidata): logic ∀ ∃ ¬ ∧ ∨ ⇒ ⇔ (7); sets ∈ ∉ ∅ ⊆ ⊂ ∪ ∩ ∖ and the number sets ℕ ℤ ℚ ℝ ℂ (13); relations and geometry ≠ ≈ ∝ ≤ ≥, congruence modulo n, divides, parallel and angle (9); operators ∑ ∏ √ ± |x| ⌊x⌋ ⌈x⌉ n! ∘ ∫ ∂ ∇ (12); the constants π, e and i and the symbol ∞ (4). Each is a symbol a student meets in upper-secondary or first-year university mathematics and has one standard meaning given the front. Where usage differs the card states the convention in its note: ⊂ is a proper subset (with ⊆ for subset), as in the Swedish Wikipedia list and the reading that pairs ⊂ with ⊆, and ℕ includes 0, as in ISO 80000-2 and Skolverket's terminology. Left out: ⊥ (two characters, and also \"false\"), ≪ and ≫, ∄, ∃!, ∴, ∵, ∮, and symbols whose meaning depends on context without a disambiguating expression (≡ alone, |, ~, ×, Δ, →)."@en ,
                 "Licensing: The only content source is Wikidata, whose structured data is CC0: the link from each symbol to its concept, the concept names in English and Swedish and the ISO 80000-2 item records come from it, and the card texts are the compiler's own wording of the meanings, so the deck is CC0 1.0. The Unicode Character Database (Unicode License v3, permissive but not a licence the builder knows), ISO 80000-2 (all rights reserved), the English Wikipedia glossary and article and the Swedish Wikipedia list and articles (CC BY-SA 4.0) were used for verification only: they confirmed the meanings and that the Swedish readings the compiler chose are the usual ones. The Swedish readings that Wikidata does not hold (\"för alla\", \"är ett element i\", \"mängddifferens\" …) are standard terminology, single words or set phrases that are not protected expression; no sentence, note, example, table layout or selection was copied from any of these sources, and notes whose first drafts followed a Wikipedia sentence closely were rewritten in round 3. The selection follows the editor's brief, grouped broadly as ISO 80000-2's clauses as recorded in Wikidata; it is not the selection of the Swedish or English Wikipedia list. Meanings of symbols and the names of mathematical concepts are facts, not copyrightable expression."@en .

<#review-0>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Sources, licences and machine checks: every symbol's character against the Unicode Character Database, its meaning against Wikidata and the English Wikipedia glossary, the Swedish terms against Wikidata sv labels and the Swedish Wikipedia; ambiguity of each front."@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 45 cards, the sources and the deck metadata."@en ,
                 "Outcome: All 45 meanings agree between Wikidata and at least one independent source (the English glossary for 41; the ISO preview and the Swedish Wikipedia for π, e and i; the Unicode character name for ∠), and every front character was confirmed in UnicodeData.txt. ISO 80000-2's tables could not be read, so ISO is cited only through Wikidata's records and the preview's clause 4. Three Wikidata checks were left out because Wikidata (or its Query Service) disagrees with the card; eight fronts were given a short expression to remove ambiguity; ⊥, ≪ and ≫ were left out."@en .

<#review-1>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Factual accuracy"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 45 cards: every front's code point and character name (Python unicodedata), the Wikidata items used in the checks (fetched live), the meanings and notes against the English and Swedish Wikipedia, and the deck description."@en ,
                 "Outcome: No factual errors in the 45 cards; three optional suggestions. Every code point and character name matched, the Wikidata items behind the checks are the intended concepts, and the notes and both stated conventions (⊂ as proper subset, 0 ∈ ℕ) are correct. All three suggestions were verified and applied."@en .

<#review-2>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Language, translation and language tags"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 45 cards plus the title, description and keywords: language tags, Swedish terms against Wikidata sv labels and Swedish Wikipedia, idiom and consistency of the English and Swedish text."@en ,
                 "Outcome: All language tags are right (every front zxx, all other text en/sv, nothing untagged) and the Swedish terms match Wikidata and Swedish Wikipedia. One warning, the composition back naming the functions in the reverse order, was confirmed and fixed; the consistency and idiom suggestions were applied, the article style of the bare-symbol backs was kept on purpose."@en .

<#review-3>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Licensing, attribution and documentation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: Every source's licence page fetched again, spot checks of the evidence quotes (17 glossary entries, 12 Swedish list rows, 7 Swedish articles, 12 UnicodeData lines, 10 Wikidata items), the required method text, and the reproducibility of the method and queries."@en ,
                 "Outcome: The recorded licences are right and Wikidata (CC0) is the only content source, so the CC0 deck licence is consistent. Three warnings were confirmed and fixed: the documentation credited Swedish readings to a verification-only CC BY-SA source, the Swedish floor note followed Swedish Wikipedia's sentence and example, and several recorded steps read files no recorded command created. The smaller attribution points were applied within what the dossier can change."@en .

<#review-4>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Re-check of changed cards and a sample (facts, language, documentation)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: 29 cards: the 17 cards changed in round 3 and every third card (for-all, and, if-and-only-if, empty-set, union, natural-numbers, real-numbers, not-equal, greater-or-equal, divides, parallel-to, product, plus-minus, floor, composition, partial-derivative, e); all 148 Wikidata checks against live Wikidata, the build's sync check, the SHACL and DCAT-AP validators, the language tags, the description, keywords, licensing and the required method text."@en ,
                 "Outcome: No errors or warnings; every fix claimed in rounds 1–3 is present. Two suggestions, both confirmed and applied: the ceiling card's Swedish Wikipedia evidence now quotes the sentence that defines takfunktionen, and the first method paragraph names the Unicode Character Database check."@en .

<#review-5>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Final full-deck review (facts and language)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 45 cards (front characters against Python unicodedata, meanings, notes, examples, Swedish terms, language tags), the description and its counts, keywords, study direction, the required method text, the pinned UnicodeData.txt URL and the fixes logged in round 4."@en ,
                 "Outcome: No errors or warnings; both round 4 fixes confirmed in place. Two suggestions, both confirmed and applied: the proportional-to note now matches its Swedish Wikipedia source, and the selection calls the third group \"relations and geometry\"."@en .

